/// United States 529 college savings plans.
///
/// A 529 plan is a tax-deferred account funded with after-tax money. What was
/// put in is its basis, and the growth is untaxed while it stays inside, and
/// untaxed when it comes out to pay a qualified education expense. Anything
/// else that comes out has its earnings taxed and a 10% tax added.
///
/// A withdrawal relieves contributions and earnings pro rata, so its earnings
/// are the plan's earnings in the same proportion (Form 1099-Q). Spend from a
/// plan straight into a place of kind `education`, and the withdrawal is
/// qualified.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, Rev. Proc. for the annual gift-tax exclusion) before relying on
/// them, and correct them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us/529

use us


/// A 529 qualified tuition program (IRC §529).
kind 529-plan : tax-deferred
  select prorata
  liquidity 5d
  has beneficiary entity

  /// Contributions above the annual gift-tax exclusion count as a gift to the
  /// beneficiary, which may need a gift-tax return (Form 709, IRC §2503(b)).
  /// This deposit took the year's contributions over the exclusion. A gift of
  /// up to five years of the exclusion may be made at once by electing to
  /// spread it (IRC §529(c)(2)(B)); if you have, this warning can be waived
  /// with `!`.
  law gift-exclusion
    on in
    when not from is income | tax-deferred
    warn total(in, year) <= gift-exclusion[year] "529 contributions over the annual gift exclusion"

  /// The earnings in a withdrawal that did not pay a qualified education
  /// expense are taxable income (IRC §529(c)(3)(A)), counted here as a
  /// distribution. Paying an education place straight from the plan, or moving
  /// the money to another 529 plan, is qualified.
  law nonqualified-income
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is education | 529-plan
    count gain as distributions
    count gain as agi

  /// A withdrawal that did not pay a qualified education expense owes an
  /// additional 10% of its earnings (IRC §529(c)(6), §530(d)(4)). This
  /// withdrawal went somewhere other than a place of kind `education`, so it is
  /// priced at that 10%. To reimburse yourself for something the plan should
  /// have paid, or when an exception applies (a scholarship, a death or
  /// disability of the beneficiary), keep the withdrawal and mark it `!` with
  /// the reason.
  law nonqualified-penalty
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    require to is education | 529-plan else owe 10% * gain to irs as nonqualified-529-penalty "529 withdrawal that did not pay an education expense"


// The annual gift-tax exclusion per recipient (IRC §2503(b)).
// Rev. Proc. 2023-34 (2024), Rev. Proc. 2024-40 (2025), Rev. Proc. 2025-32 (2026).
// verify: the 2026 figure against Rev. Proc. 2025-32.
param gift-exclusion
  2024 18_000 USD
  2025 19_000 USD
  2026 19_000 USD
